#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad\ny = t^2-t, \\qquad\nz = t^2-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =i+x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+(-{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}+t \\\\  & =3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf2943fc-f134-53a9-994e-b427709ccf39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130949} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f799a3-f0e5-55d2-ba9e-bfe3224a4cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130950} {:answer/id #uuid "26fae208-019b-555a-99b1-0bcb29c38820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130951} {:answer/id #uuid "51f21ab6-f636-592c-9370-671a399beb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130952} {:answer/id #uuid "602c1799-3e61-52e6-8f53-9f39f1b26950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130953}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d067bdc7-90c8-523d-8b30-6ea34aea59b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77457099-bf92-5a80-b30d-5158400cc17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371989} {:answer/id #uuid "114e3ccc-e21f-52db-b19d-3f3897328e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371990} {:answer/id #uuid "57a39c82-1760-5e0d-ab2f-00a266a5b5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371991} {:answer/id #uuid "52460aa2-bb42-52a1-ba47-8cd7c05b7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371992} {:answer/id #uuid "0670939e-8e1b-5069-8ab1-36c39e6a1734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77457099-bf92-5a80-b30d-5158400cc17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371989} :answer-field/id #uuid "1918f620-1ef5-50a5-bd08-efba031c5de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab9a09b7-00f2-517e-bc29-69739ea5a8ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-161928" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+(t-{t}^{3})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=t-{t}^{3},\\,z=2{t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-{t}^{3})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(1-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(4t-2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t-{t}^{3})\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(1-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(4t-2)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (1-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d95993-6fce-50a6-9da0-c38e0b9621ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130961} {:answer/id #uuid "02754b0a-e75b-5997-be48-1296815d866a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130962} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0bbee9-caa1-52ab-a326-73150026e797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130963} {:answer/id #uuid "1e11ae00-c494-5174-8b2a-aa2dab840740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130964} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48ccdf-94f7-5b30-b9e9-c2b9d97a02fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130965}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbd2886-ea55-5ec0-b21b-dda63331dd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130960}] :question/id #uuid "008f670b-b77a-5939-9c4f-8602d138cc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162377" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t) = \\sin (πt)i + \\cos (πt)j + \\sin (πt)k$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1)$.\nGiven that $F(x, y, z) = xi + yk$, evaluate the integral\n$∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27840c28-45a9-5916-b4c1-d818db73736e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278944} {:answer/id #uuid "70f20afe-3ba7-5528-8071-2b9eaa046011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279616} {:answer/id #uuid "a723a97b-4d1b-542c-b720-ec65cd2786a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} {:answer/id #uuid "ddabc804-5099-5814-b0bc-e09a67f7767a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280545} {:answer/id #uuid "e94716b8-3388-57e0-901d-e8160a0aacf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-5}{103}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a723a97b-4d1b-542c-b720-ec65cd2786a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} :answer-field/id #uuid "559ef114-250d-5d31-9814-ea273f22c26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e15967c-9185-5388-a8b6-baaf9a1de47a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162316" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j+({t}^{3}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+3t,\\,z={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (6t+3)+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{6}+\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}+1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125e28c0-bbb7-5d93-95b9-69fe92e1f551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278759} {:answer/id #uuid "3eefc9e2-c929-54c3-9081-01b14d2b4379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279147} {:answer/id #uuid "5764f77b-9cd0-5e34-8a78-c0ca6ff9d828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279393} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7cfdf-beb6-5886-9078-290c6cf747ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} {:answer/id #uuid "dad10f5c-6829-52c8-b539-d1d5a5320bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7cfdf-beb6-5886-9078-290c6cf747ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f8a8c0-e049-5f88-9126-dfef6847e2bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280965}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "268c6ade-bfb9-5104-a3ea-6d4f751fe4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162324" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d80d2d-e154-51fd-a594-5eba0a5e6b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278765} {:answer/id #uuid "14b8499c-89bd-5bc3-931b-c40e7ce37aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278777} {:answer/id #uuid "72b205e0-a52e-5333-be4c-5c18dbe481a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3aff6a-8638-5f77-8507-0d3f55b19bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280102} {:answer/id #uuid "f732e6da-e394-595c-9b7c-3f0005986060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f732e6da-e394-595c-9b7c-3f0005986060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea93f9f9-61f9-5ba5-a529-0a7b5d54ca7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "915ccbd6-d5b8-5b79-ba41-8aee577baac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162886" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a49d17-1ccd-5d21-8067-3cc28c01dfc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279659} {:answer/id #uuid "9498af25-8e31-5391-9c5d-57b5a039adb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279913} {:answer/id #uuid "9af2af40-8464-54ca-9c05-b548baff602f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279964} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c29f02-dc00-5dc7-805e-51068b29a448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} {:answer/id #uuid "e84e4b95-0c02-5828-a503-1bc5e276aa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3c29f02-dc00-5dc7-805e-51068b29a448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} :answer-field/id #uuid "98a966d4-0bb1-5c55-b273-7cce49dd0df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c852df75-6989-5a7d-bc7f-0618226da77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162887" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-1)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k)\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5b07f3-4813-5a4a-a0e6-a5bf77edd48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278852} {:answer/id #uuid "24161e0a-0b08-5db9-9481-589c38c2931e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278915} {:answer/id #uuid "3927e74a-4b53-5c18-92e5-d51492930769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279093} {:answer/id #uuid "bc856658-afbb-5fe8-bdf2-8d82839c6b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280249} {:answer/id #uuid "f923d8f6-fbaa-5b3e-a1a9-91366f82223e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc856658-afbb-5fe8-bdf2-8d82839c6b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280249} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cbe268-3f0b-5f24-95dd-226717656d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "203be68d-096d-564c-bf44-7d62e44d7418" :question/math-academy-id "q-162892" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+1\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-2\\pi t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2\\pi (1)+0)-(-2\\pi (-1)+0) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45117c8-92b5-5e85-be68-59207cfdcaeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372211} {:answer/id #uuid "94d1fa98-7766-5aa8-ae09-9d3addbd727b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372212} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec03585-7795-522f-a5f2-67e2d22d5768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372213} {:answer/id #uuid "ce20f1dc-7964-554d-81c6-eddb4382c3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372214} {:answer/id #uuid "fae3e8e3-085f-5a58-b783-baab0dfc6066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce20f1dc-7964-554d-81c6-eddb4382c3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372214} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4d8f220-0d83-53ee-9569-d72e7c144c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48c5ec06-77b9-5c6d-9d25-1b845d8b6bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162889" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+3x\\,j+2{y}^{2}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+3x\\,j+2{y}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi )+0)-(1+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+0) \\\\  & =3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f568a2f-7b80-5f85-b384-1c1a684d00a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372218} {:answer/id #uuid "db5f892d-b188-5614-98ed-59c650396111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372219} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb21bec-4585-5873-bda2-04c6a3caa80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372220} {:answer/id #uuid "37824a70-c1a6-564e-97d8-b4dfb038f620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372221} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2d7d23-39ba-56a6-a28d-95d8896f320a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceb21bec-4585-5873-bda2-04c6a3caa80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372220} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e3f2827-69ad-5c26-8f65-1978187ebe9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eadc1c42-fade-50b5-8c58-c8e95e3536a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162891" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=(y-z)\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+(x-y)\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-z)\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+(x-y)\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(-1+0-2(2\\pi ))-(-1+0-2(0)) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431148} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431161} {:answer/id #uuid "9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431162} {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} {:answer/id #uuid "f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431180}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162999" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,z={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+3y\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =(-3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+\\frac{13}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3+\\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431154} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431163} {:answer/id #uuid "ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431164} {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} {:answer/id #uuid "e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} :answer-field/id #uuid "db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162314" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=t+{t}^{2},\\,z=-t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (1+2t)+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\cdot (-1-2t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}+{t}^{4}+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{2}{5}+1-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd3c9ec-3cf7-567b-a353-7585d49bb4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130967} {:answer/id #uuid "011715b2-f35f-5761-81b5-48d58c2b0ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6bded5-6334-5d42-977c-94cb680710d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130969} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2e7185-13ca-551c-b2b9-a1f06717c040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130970} {:answer/id #uuid "7c572ba2-1637-5089-bb75-49b67db7af89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130971}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7d720396-d9d8-5526-a6c9-d783c2ddfa07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130966}] :question/id #uuid "9360d1cf-9517-569c-8e77-dfd2eb8609da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162407" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = xzi + y^{2}j + k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}A A A A Adzdydx$.\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b4e90d-c970-54c3-ab06-a30ba09864e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278607} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe8543-ec3e-587e-9768-a831766e3eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} {:answer/id #uuid "acad9a07-0f5d-5457-86b1-82efec638ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280120} {:answer/id #uuid "ea617dc5-3c2b-587f-bfa4-9046fb77867d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ee8cb1-a891-5ada-b9cf-ae248d766e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152cbc4e-4f16-538e-9423-127439b0fd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162408" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100640c1-bb74-5261-b07b-162818bcec64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278736} {:answer/id #uuid "612e775d-f4d3-5491-a568-8632b1dc20b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279479} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e024e7-b5ac-5532-a30e-fcebd4a57c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280046} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3f43b4-3268-56c7-b7af-4bdd5b0709c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280255} {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,